{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_001",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "As she gazed into those gentle blue eyes, engaged in conversation, and devised various plans, the evening slipped away at an extraordinary pace. Before she knew it, the supper table, which traditionally marked the end of such gatherings, was set and ready, having been moved to the fireside. With an enthusiasm that surpassed her usual eagerness to please, and a genuine desire to make a good impression, she took charge of the meal, offering and recommending the minced chicken and scalloped oysters with a persuasive urgency that she knew would be well-received by their guests, considering the early hour and their polite reservations. On such occasions, Mr. Woodhouse's feelings were torn. He had a fondness for the traditional supper, reminiscent of his youth, but his conviction that such meals were unwholesome made him regret seeing the table laden with food. While his hospitality inclined him to offer his visitors every courtesy, his concern for their health made him wish they would abstain from eating. The only dish he could wholeheartedly recommend was a small basin of thin gruel, similar to his own. However, as the ladies enjoyed the finer dishes, he might suggest, 'Mrs. Bates, might I propose you try one of these eggs? A softly boiled egg is not harmful. Serle is particularly skilled at boiling eggs, and I would not recommend anyone else's. But please, do not be alarmed, they are quite small. Miss Bates, allow Emma to serve you a small portion of tart - a very small portion, I assure you. Our tarts are all made with apples, and you need not worry about unwholesome preserves. I must advise against the custard, though. Mrs. Goddard, would you care for half a glass of wine, diluted with water, of course?' Emma humored her father, but attended to her guests in a far more satisfactory manner, taking great pleasure in seeing them depart happy. Miss Smith's happiness was equal to Emma's intentions. As a prominent figure in Highbury, Miss Woodhouse's introduction had initially caused Miss Smith both excitement and trepidation, but the kind and gracious manner in which she was treated throughout the evening left her feeling deeply grateful, and she was even honored with a handshake upon her departure. Harriet Smith soon became a regular visitor to Hartfield. Emma, being decisive and prompt in her actions, lost no time in inviting, encouraging, and welcoming her to visit often. As their acquaintance grew, so did their mutual satisfaction. Emma had initially recognized the potential benefits of having Harriet as a walking companion, a role that had become more important since Mrs. Weston's departure. Emma's father rarely ventured beyond the shrubbery, where the changing seasons dictated the length of his walks, and since Mrs. Weston's loss, Emma had been seeking a suitable replacement."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_001",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "Emma was so caught up in talking and listening to her guests that the evening flew by quickly. Before she knew it, the supper table was set and ready to be moved to the fire. She made sure to do everything well and attentively, helping her guests with the food and recommending the minced chicken and scalloped oysters. She knew her guests would appreciate her efforts, especially given the early hour and their polite manners. Mr. Woodhouse, Emma's father, was torn about the supper. He liked the tradition of having a supper, but he was worried that the food might not be good for his guests' health. He would have been happy to have them eat anything, but at the same time, he was concerned about the potential harm. All he could bring himself to recommend was a small bowl of thin gruel, which he thought was a safe choice. As the ladies were eating, he suggested that Mrs. Bates try one of the eggs, which he thought was a harmless option. He also offered Miss Bates a small piece of tart and asked Mrs. Goddard if she would like a little bit of wine mixed with water. Emma let her father talk, but she made sure to take care of her guests in a more practical way. She was happy to see them leave with full plates and big smiles. Miss Smith, in particular, was thrilled with how the evening had gone. She had been a bit nervous about meeting Emma, but Emma had been very kind and friendly. As the days went by, Harriet Smith became a regular visitor to Hartfield, Emma's home. Emma had quickly taken a liking to her and encouraged her to come over often. As they spent more time together, they grew closer and closer. Emma had been looking for a walking companion, and Harriet fit the bill perfectly. Since her father didn't like to walk far, Emma had been missing having someone to explore with. With Harriet by her side, she could finally do that again."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_002",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "When Emma was fourteen, she made a list of books to read. I thought it showed good judgment, so I kept it for a while. Now, I don't expect her to stick to any plan that requires effort and patience. Miss Taylor couldn't get her to read much, and I'm sure Harriet Smith won't be able to either. You know Emma never liked to read as much as you wanted her to. Mrs. Weston said, 'I dare say I thought so back then, but since we've been apart, I don't remember Emma ever not doing something I asked.' Mr. Knightley replied, 'There's no need to remind me of that. But I, who haven't been charmed by her, still see and remember things. Emma has been spoiled because she's the smartest in her family. At ten, she could answer questions that puzzled her sister at seventeen. She's always been confident, while her sister Isabella is slow and unsure. Since she was twelve, Emma has been in charge of the house and everyone in it. When her mother died, she lost the only person who could have controlled her. Emma inherited her mother's talents and was under her guidance. Mrs. Weston said, 'I'm glad I didn't have to rely on your recommendation if I had left Mr. Woodhouse's family and needed a new job. I don't think you would have said anything good about me to anyone.' Mr. Knightley smiled and said, 'You're better off here. You're a good wife, but you wouldn't have been a good governess. While you were at Hartfield, you were learning how to be a good wife by following Emma's lead and doing what you were told. If Weston had asked me to recommend a wife, I would have suggested you.' Mrs. Weston replied, 'It's not hard to be a good wife to someone like Mr. Weston.' Mr. Knightley said, 'To be honest, I think you might be wasted on him. He's a comfortable man, and you might not have any challenges to face. But we'll see. Maybe Weston will become grumpy because he's too comfortable, or his son will cause trouble.' Mrs. Weston said, 'I hope not. It's unlikely that his son would cause problems.' Mr. Knightley said, 'I'm just mentioning possibilities. I don't claim to be able to predict the future like Emma does. I hope the young man will be like his father in character and like his mother's family in wealth. But Harriet Smith - I haven't even started talking about her. I think she's the worst possible companion for Emma."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_004",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "And should she, at the tender age of seventeen, be expected to accept the first proposal that comes her way, simply because she is now entering society and beginning to attract attention? No, surely she deserves time to consider her options and become acquainted with the world. \"I have always considered their close relationship to be most unwise,\" Mr. Knightley remarked after a pause, \"although I have kept my thoughts to myself until now. However, I fear it will ultimately prove to be a most unfortunate association for Harriet. You will fill her head with exaggerated notions of her own beauty and the high expectations she should have, and soon she will deem no one worthy of her attention. Vanity can have a profound impact on a weak and impressionable mind, leading to all sorts of trouble. It is all too easy for a young woman to set her sights too high. Although Harriet Smith is a very attractive girl, she may not receive as many proposals as she might hope. Sensible men do not typically seek out frivolous wives, and men of means are often hesitant to form connections with someone of uncertain background. Most prudent men would be wary of the potential inconvenience and scandal that could arise when the truth about her parentage is revealed. If she were to marry Robert Martin, she would be ensured a life of respectability, safety, and happiness; however, if you encourage her to aim higher and expect a grand marriage to a man of great fortune and standing, she may find herself a permanent resident at Mrs. Goddard's boarding house, or, at the very least, remain unwed until she becomes desperate and settles for someone like the old writing-master's son. \"We hold such differing opinions on this matter, Mr. Knightley, that it seems pointless to discuss it further, as we will only succeed in irritating each other. As for my allowing her to marry Robert Martin, it is no longer a possibility, for she has already refused him in a most decisive manner, making any future proposal unlikely. She must now live with the consequences of her decision, and as for the refusal itself, I will not deny that I may have exerted some influence over her; however, I must assure you that there was little for me or anyone else to do, given his unappealing appearance and unpleasant demeanor. If she had never encountered anyone superior, she might have tolerated him, particularly since he is the brother of her friends and had made an effort to please her. Nevertheless, her perspective has changed now that she has been exposed to more refined company, and she can recognize the difference between a true gentleman and someone like Robert Martin. \"Such nonsense, absolute nonsense!\" exclaimed Mr. Knightley. \"Robert Martin's manners are characterized by sense, sincerity, and good humor, and his mind possesses a genuine gentility that far surpasses Harriet Smith's understanding.\" Emma remained silent, attempting to appear cheerful and unconcerned, but in reality, she felt uneasy and wished for Mr. Knightley to take his leave."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_004",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "And is she, at seventeen, just starting to experience life and being admired because she doesn't accept the first offer she gets? No, let her take her time to look around. Mr. Knightley said, 'I have always thought it was a bad idea for you to be so close to Harriet, but I never said anything. Now I think it will be bad for her. You will give her big ideas about her beauty and what she deserves, and soon nobody will be good enough for her. When vanity takes over a weak person, it causes all sorts of problems. It's easy for a young woman to have high expectations. Harriet Smith might not get many marriage offers, even though she's pretty. Smart men don't want silly wives, and men from good families don't want to marry someone with an unknown past. If she marries Robert Martin, she'll be safe, respected, and happy. But if you encourage her to expect a rich and important husband, she might end up living with Mrs. Goddard forever, or at least until she gets desperate and marries someone she doesn't really want to. Mr. Knightley and I disagree on this, so there's no point in discussing it. As for Harriet marrying Robert Martin, it's impossible - she's already turned him down, and I don't think he'll ask again. She'll have to deal with the consequences of saying no, and I won't pretend I didn't influence her decision a little. But really, there wasn't much anyone could do. Robert Martin doesn't look or act very appealing, and if she ever liked him, she doesn't now. Before she met other people, she might have tolerated him because he's the brother of her friends and tried to please her. But now she knows what real gentlemen are like, and only someone with a good education and manners has a chance with her. Mr. Knightley said, 'That's nonsense! Robert Martin's manners are sensible, sincere, and friendly, and he has more class than Harriet could understand.' Emma didn't respond and tried to look calm, but she was feeling uncomfortable and wanted Mr. Knightley to leave."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_006",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "Papa is merely expressing his own regret, Emma thought. 'Very much to the honour of both,' was the gracious response. Isabella then asked her father in a melancholy tone, 'And do you see her, sir, fairly often?' Mr. Woodhouse hesitated before answering, 'Not as often as I would like, my dear.' Isabella's mother countered, 'Oh, papa, we have only gone one whole day without seeing them since their marriage. Either in the morning or evening of every day, except one, we have seen either Mr. Weston or Mrs. Weston, and usually both, either at Randalls or here - and as you can imagine, Isabella, mostly here. They are extremely kind to us in their visits. Mr. Weston is just as kind as his wife. Papa, if you speak in such a melancholy way, you will give Isabella a false impression of us all. Everyone must be aware that Miss Taylor is missed, but everyone should also be assured that Mr. and Mrs. Weston prevent us from missing her to a great extent - which is the truth.' 'Just as it should be,' said Mr. John Knightley, 'and just as I had hoped from your letters. Her desire to show you attention could not be doubted, and his being a sociable man makes it easy. I have always told you, my love, that I did not think the change would affect Hartfield as much as you feared; and now you have Emma's account, I hope you will be satisfied.' 'To be sure,' said Mr. Woodhouse, 'yes, certainly - I must admit that Mrs. Weston, poor Mrs. Weston, does visit us quite often - but then she always has to leave again.' 'It would be very hard on Mr. Weston if she did not, papa,' Emma said. 'You quite forget poor Mr. Weston.' John Knightley chimed in pleasantly, 'I think, indeed, that Mr. Weston has some claim. You and I, Emma, will take the part of the poor husband. As for Isabella, she has been married long enough to see the convenience of considering all the Mr. Westons as much as she can.' 'Me, my love,' cried his wife, only partially understanding, 'are you talking about me? I am sure nobody ought to be, or can be, a greater advocate for marriage than I am; and if it had not been for the misery of Miss Taylor leaving Hartfield, I would never have thought of her as anything but the most fortunate woman in the world. And as for slighting Mr. Weston, that excellent Mr. Weston, I think he deserves nothing but praise. I believe he is one of the most even-tempered men that ever existed. Except for you and your brother, I do not know his equal for temper. I shall never forget his kindness in flying Henry's kite for him on that very windy day last Easter, and ever since his thoughtful note last September, written at midnight to assure me there was no scarlet fever at Cobham, I have been convinced there could not be a more feeling heart nor a better man in existence."
}

