  "condition": "modernize",
  "rewritten_text": "Papa is only expressing his own regret. Very much to the credit of both, was the kind response. And do you see her, sir, fairly often, asked Isabella in the melancholic tone that suited her father. Mr. Woodhouse hesitated, Not as often as I'd like, my dear. Oh, papa, we've only missed seeing them one full day since they got married. Either in the morning or evening of every day, except one, we've seen either Mr. Weston or Mrs. Weston, and usually both, either at Randalls or here - and as you can imagine, Isabella, most often here. They're very kind to visit us so frequently. Mr. Weston is really as kind as his wife. Papa, if you speak in that sad way, you'll give Isabella the wrong idea about all of us. Everyone must know that Miss Taylor will be missed, but everyone should also be assured that Mr. and Mrs. Weston prevent us from missing her as much as we thought we would - which is the truth. Just as it should be, said Mr. John Knightley, and just as I hoped from your letters. Her desire to show you attention couldn't be doubted, and his being a social and available man makes it all easy. I've been telling you, my love, that I didn't think the change would affect Hartfield as much as you thought; and now you have Emma's account, I hope you'll be satisfied. Why, to be sure, said Mr. Woodhouse, yes, certainly - I won't deny that Mrs. Weston, poor Mrs. Weston, does come to see us pretty often - but then she always has to leave again. It would be really tough on Mr. Weston if she didn't, papa - you're forgetting about poor Mr. Weston. I think, indeed, said John Knightley pleasantly, that Mr. Weston has a good point. You and I, Emma, will take the side of the husband. I, being a husband, and you not being a wife, probably see the claims of the man with equal importance. As for Isabella, she's been married long enough to know the convenience of prioritizing her husband. Me, my love, cried his wife, hearing and understanding only partially - are you talking about me? I'm sure nobody should be, or can be, a greater supporter of marriage than I am; and if it hadn't been for the sadness of Miss Taylor leaving Hartfield, I would never have thought of her as anything but the most fortunate woman in the world; and as for neglecting Mr. Weston, that excellent Mr. Weston, I think he deserves all the appreciation - I believe he's one of the most even-tempered men that ever existed. Except for you and your brother, I don't know anyone with a better temper. I'll never forget him flying Henry's kite for him on that very windy day last Easter - and since his kind gesture last September, writing that note at midnight to reassure me there was no scarlet fever at Cobham, I've been convinced there couldn't be a more caring heart or a better man."
}

