{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_007",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "You're doing the right thing, she said. We'll apologize to Mr. and Mrs. Weston for you. But before she could say anything else, her brother offered Mr. Elton a ride in his carriage, and Mr. Elton happily accepted. It was all settled; Mr. Elton was going, and he looked very pleased. His face showed more joy than ever before, and his smile was stronger when he looked at her next. She thought to herself, This is strange. After I made sure he didn't have to go, he chose to go out and leave Harriet behind when she's not feeling well. It's odd. Many single men, including Mr. Elton, seem to love going out to dinner. It's one of their favorite things to do, and they can't say no to an invitation. Mr. Elton is a great guy, and he really likes Harriet, but he can't resist a dinner invitation. It's weird how love works; he can see how funny Harriet is, but he won't give up dinner plans for her. Soon after, Mr. Elton left, and she had to admit that he seemed very sincere when he said goodbye. He promised to visit Harriet and see how she was doing, and he seemed genuinely concerned. He smiled and sighed as he left, which made her think well of him. After a few minutes of silence, John Knightley spoke up. I've never seen a man try so hard to be nice as Mr. Elton, he said. When he's around women, he works really hard to be charming. With men, he's normal and relaxed, but with women, he tries too hard. Emma replied, Mr. Elton's manners aren't perfect, but when someone is trying to be nice, you should overlook their flaws. If someone is doing their best with what they have, they'll do better than someone who is lazy but has more natural talent. Mr. Elton is a good guy, and you have to appreciate his good nature. John Knightley said with a hint of teasing, It seems like he has a lot of good feelings towards you. Emma smiled in surprise and said, You think Mr. Elton likes me? John Knightley said, I've thought about it, and you should consider it too. Emma laughed and said, Mr. Elton in love with me? That's a crazy idea. John Knightley said, I'm not saying it's true, but you should think about it and be careful how you act around him. I think you've been encouraging him."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_008",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "He had walked beyond the sweep, some way down the Highbury road, where the snow was barely half an inch deep and hardly covered the ground in many places. Only a few flakes were falling at the moment, but the clouds were clearing, suggesting the storm would soon pass. He had spoken with the coachmen, and they shared his opinion that there was no cause for concern. This news brought great relief to Isabella, and Emma was also pleased to see her father's anxiety eased, although his nervous nature still made him uneasy about staying at Randalls. He was convinced it was safe to return home, but no amount of reassurance could calm his fears about remaining. While the others discussed and debated, Mr. Knightley and Emma quickly settled the matter. 'Your father will not be at ease; why not go?' 'I am ready, if the others are.' 'Shall I ring the bell?' 'Yes, do.' The bell was rung, and the carriages were summoned. Emma hoped that soon she would see one troublesome companion safely at home, where he could sober up and calm down, and the other could recover his temper once the difficult visit was over. The carriage arrived, and Mr. Woodhouse, always the first to be attended to on such occasions, was carefully helped into his own carriage by Mr. Knightley and Mr. Weston. However, despite their efforts to reassure him, the sight of the snow and the darker night than expected caused him to become alarmed again. 'He was afraid they would have a terrible drive. He was afraid poor Isabella would not like it. And there was poor Emma in the carriage behind. He did not know what they should do. They must stay together as much as possible;' and James was instructed to drive slowly and wait for the other carriage. Isabella got in after her father, followed by John Knightley, who naturally got in after his wife, so that when Emma was escorted into the second carriage by Mr. Elton, she found herself in a tête-à-tête situation. This would not have been awkward under normal circumstances, and she could have enjoyed talking to him about Harriet, making the three-quarters of a mile seem like nothing. But now, she wished it had not happened. She suspected he had had too much of Mr. Weston's wine and would likely talk nonsense. To restrain him as much as possible, she prepared to speak with great calmness and gravity about the weather and the night, but barely had she started, barely had they passed the sweep-gate and joined the other carriage, than she found her conversation interrupted, her hand taken, and her attention demanded."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_008",
  "condition": "modernize",
  "rewritten_text": "He had walked beyond the curve of the road, a bit along the Highbury road. The snow was nowhere more than half an inch deep, and in many places, it was barely enough to cover the ground. Only a few flakes were falling at the moment, but the clouds were clearing, and it seemed like the snow would be stopping soon. He had spoken with the coachmen, and they both agreed with him that there was nothing to worry about. The news was a huge relief to Isabella, and it was almost as welcome to Emma, who was concerned about her father's well-being. He was immediately reassured, as much as his nervous nature allowed, but the alarm that had been raised couldn't be calmed as long as he was still at Randalls. He was convinced that it was safe to go home, but no amount of reassurance could persuade him that it was safe to stay. While the others were discussing and debating, Mr. Knightley and Emma quickly made a decision. 'Your father won't be at ease, so why don't you go?' 'I'm ready if the others are.' 'Should I call for the car?' 'Yes, do.' The car arrived, and Emma hoped to soon see one troublesome companion dropped off at his own house, where he could sober up and calm down, and the other recover his temper and happiness once this difficult visit was over. The car came, and Mr. Woodhouse, always the first priority in situations like this, was carefully helped into his own car by Mr. Knightley and Mr. Weston. However, despite their efforts, Mr. Woodhouse couldn't help but feel a renewed sense of alarm when he saw the snow that had fallen and realized it was much darker than he had expected. 'He was afraid they would have a very bad drive. He was worried that poor Isabella wouldn't like it. And there was poor Emma in the car behind. He didn't know what they should do. They should try to stay together as much as possible,' he said, and James was instructed to drive slowly and wait for the other car. Isabella got in after her father, and John Knightley, forgetting that he wasn't part of their group, naturally got in after his wife. As a result, Emma found herself escorted into the second car by Mr. Elton, and the door was closed, leaving them alone for the drive. It wouldn't have been awkward at all, and it might have even been enjoyable, if it hadn't been for the suspicions she had developed that day. She could have talked to him about Harriet, and the three-quarters of a mile would have seemed like nothing. But now, she wished it hadn't happened. She thought he had had too much of Mr. Weston's good wine and was sure he would want to talk nonsense. To keep him in check as much as possible, she prepared to speak with extreme calmness and seriousness about the weather and the night. However, she had barely started, and they had just passed the curve and joined the other car, when she found her topic cut off, her hand taken, and her attention demanded by Mr."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_008",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "He had walked a bit along the Highbury road and found the snow was not very deep, only about half an inch in some places. The snowflakes were falling gently, but the clouds were clearing, and it seemed like the snow would stop soon. He had talked to the coachmen, and they agreed with him that there was nothing to worry about. This news was a big relief to Isabella, and it also made Emma feel better because her father was nervous about the weather. Even though they told him it was safe to go home, he was still worried about staying at Randalls. Mr. Knightley and Emma quickly decided that he should go home. They said, 'Your father will not be easy; why do not you go?' 'I am ready, if the others are.' 'Shall I ring the bell?' 'Yes, do.' So they called for the carriages. Emma was hoping to get one of her annoying companions to leave and calm down, and the other to regain his good mood after this difficult visit. The carriage arrived, and Mr. Woodhouse was helped into his own carriage by Mr. Knightley and Mr. Weston. But even with their reassurance, he was still worried when he saw the snow and how dark it was. He was afraid they would have a bad drive and that Isabella would not like it. He was also worried about Emma in the other carriage. He told James to drive slowly and wait for the other carriage. Isabella got into the carriage after her father, and John Knightley got in after her. Emma was escorted into the second carriage by Mr. Elton, and they were left alone. This would have been nice before, but now Emma was not happy about it. She thought Mr. Elton had drunk too much wine and would talk nonsense. To avoid this, she tried to speak calmly and seriously about the weather, but Mr. Elton interrupted her and took her hand, demanding her attention."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_009",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "As he regained his composure, he should have taken control and dismissed the unworthy aspects of their influence. He should have resisted their initial attempts to make him disregard his father. Had he started as he should have, the current situation would not be problematic. 'We will never see eye to eye on this matter,' Emma exclaimed. 'However, that is not unusual. I do not think he is a weak young man; I am convinced he is not. Mr. Weston would not be oblivious to foolishness, even in his own son. Nevertheless, he is likely to have a more accommodating and gentle nature than you would consider ideal for a man. I suspect he does, and although it may deprive him of certain advantages, it will bring him many others. 'Yes, all the advantages of inaction when he should be taking action, and of leading a life of mere indulgence and leisure, convincing himself that he has discovered the perfect method for maintaining peace at home and avoiding his father's criticism,' Mr. Knightley replied. 'He can sit down and write an elaborate, flowery letter filled with empty promises and falsehoods, persuading himself that he has found the best way to preserve harmony and prevent his father from having any grounds for complaint. His letters are distasteful to me.' 'Your sentiments are unusual. They seem to satisfy everyone else.' 'I doubt they satisfy Mrs. Weston. They can hardly satisfy a woman of her intelligence and sensitivity, who, although not his mother, is in a maternal position and therefore not blinded by maternal affection. It is for her sake that attention to Randalls is especially important, and she must feel the omission even more keenly. Had she been a person of greater importance, he would have come, and it would not have mattered whether he did or not. Do you think your friend is neglecting these considerations? Do you suppose she does not often think this to herself? No, Emma, your charming young man can only be charming in a superficial sense, not in a genuinely amiable way. He may have good manners and be agreeable, but he lacks true consideration for the feelings of others.' 'You seem determined to think poorly of him.' 'Not at all,' Mr. Knightley replied, somewhat displeased. 'I do not want to think ill of him. I am willing to acknowledge his merits, just like anyone else. However, I have not heard of any, except for his physical appearance and smooth, plausible manners.' 'Well, if he has nothing else to recommend him, he will be a welcome addition to Highbury. We do not often encounter fine young men who are well-bred and agreeable. We must not be overly particular and expect all the virtues at once. Can you imagine, Mr. Knightley, the sensation his arrival will cause? There will be only one topic of conversation throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury; only one subject of interest and curiosity. It will all be about Mr. Frank Churchill; we will think and speak of no one else.' 'You will excuse my being overwhelmed. If I find him to be a pleasant conversationalist, I will be glad to make his acquaintance. However, if he is merely a self-important, talkative fool, he will not occupy much of my time or thoughts.'"
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_010",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
