ked away, Wemmick told me, 'He's a Coiner, a skilled workman. The Recorder's report is due today, and he's likely to be executed on Monday. Yet, as you see, even in his situation, a pair of pigeons is still a valuable possession.' Wemmick glanced back at a dead plant, then looked around, considering which pot would fit best in its place. As we left the prison through the lodge, I noticed that the turnkeys treated my guardian with great respect, just like the prisoners. 'Well, Mr. Wemmick,' the turnkey said, 'what's Mr. Jaggers going to do about that water-side murder?'"
