  "rewritten_text": "In approximately four and a half hours, the balloon was sufficiently inflated, so I attached the car and loaded all my equipment, including the condensing apparatus, a substantial amount of water, and a generous supply of provisions like pemmican, which is rich in nutrients despite its compact size. I also placed a pair of pigeons and a cat in the car. As daybreak was approaching, I deemed it time to depart. While pretending to pick up a lit cigar I had dropped, I discreetly ignited the slow match that protruded slightly from one of the smaller casks. This action went unnoticed by the three men nearby, and I quickly jumped into the car, cut the cord that tied me to the ground, and was pleased to find that I ascended effortlessly, carrying 175 pounds of leaden ballast with ease and capable of carrying just as much more. However, I had barely reached a height of 50 yards when a violent hurricane of fire, smoke, sulfur, and debris erupted beneath me, causing my heart to sink and filling me with unbridled terror. I realized too late that I had overestimated the situation, and the full consequences of the explosion were still to come. In an instant, I felt my blood rushing to my temples, followed by a deafening concussion that seemed to tear the sky apart. Later, when I had time to reflect, I attributed the explosion's intense violence to my position directly above it, but in the moment, my only concern was survival. The balloon initially collapsed, then expanded furiously, spinning around with terrifying speed before hurling me over the car's rim, leaving me dangling at a great height, upside down, with my face outward, suspended by a slender cord that had become entangled around my left foot. The horror of my situation was indescribable - I gasped for air, my body convulsed with fear, my eyes felt like they were being torn from their sockets, and a wave of nausea washed over me before I lost consciousness. I have no idea how long I remained in this